WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.720
Vsauce!

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.920
Kevin here, and I'm an IDIOT.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.960
I am the Duke of the dull and dim-witted.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.680
I am breathtakingly brainless.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:16.160
I am fabulously fat-headed, disastrously doltish,
and confoundingly cretinous.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:23.360
I've spent 10 years making hundreds of videos
celebrating knowledge and creativity.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.360
I’ve highlighted the achievements of some
of the smartest minds in science and math.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.040
I’ve learned a lot along the way, and maybe
you have, too.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:42.880
But for the last few weeks I've been pursuing
peak stupidity -- and, uhh, it's going pretty

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:43.520
well.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:50.000
The fact is -- I hope this video actually
makes you dumber, because sometimes being

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.080
an idiot is the difference between life and
death.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:55.760
Here's how.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:57.520
Hold on.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.280
I gotta get this BBQ sauce off my face.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.520
It's like literally burning my skin.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:02.480
Okay hang on.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.000
Oh this is for a child.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.840
Okay I am going to smell like BBQ sauce for
the rest of this video but it's what I do

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:12.980
for you.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.040
Okay here’s how.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.280
In 1942, psychologist&nbsp;
Alexander Luchins administered&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.560
a problem-solving test using water and differently

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.680
sized jars.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:30.240
The goal is to fill and empty those jars with
water in a combination to get to the desired

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:30.880
volume.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.160
You’ve got an unlimited supply of water,
and you can throw out as much as you want.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:43.120
In Problem #1, Jar A holds 29 ounces and Jar
B holds 3 ounces.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.840
The goal is to measure 20 ounces.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:53.720
So, you’d fill the 29 ounce jar and use
that to fill the 3 ounce jar 3 times (29 - 3

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.880
- 3 - 3 = 20), leaving you with 20 ounces.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.200
This is not a paradox.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.840
Problem #2 adds a third jar.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:11.360
This time, Jar A is 21oz, Jar B is 127oz,
and Jar C is 3oz.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:24.160
We have to get 100oz, and that’s also pretty
easy (127 - 21 - 3 - 3 = 100).

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.680
The third problem is a little bit trickier,
but now we know how to solve these -- Jar

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:36.960
A holds 14oz, Jar B holds 163oz, and Jar C
holds 25oz.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.760
Your goal is to get 99oz.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:54.880
You can fill the 163, then pour two 25’s
and one 14 for 163 - 25 - 25 - 14 = 99.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.400
Once you’re locked in to the successful
strategy, you’re on auto-pilot.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:06.960
Fill the biggest jar and use the other two
smaller jars to reduce it until you’ve got

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.400
the right amount.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.200
Got it.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.840
Alright, one more:

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:18.800
Jar A holds 23oz, Jar B holds 49oz, Jar C
holds 3oz.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.040
We need 20oz.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:29.280
So, 49 - 23 - 3 - 3 = 20.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.000
Right?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.800
Wr… well, yes, that’s actually right.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.480
But it’s not the most efficient solution.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:45.520
We could just fill Jar A with 23 ounces and
then pour 3 ounces into Jar C and have the

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.280
20 ounces that we need.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.680
This time, we don’t need this largest jar
at all.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:59.280
Learning an algorithm to solve these problems
actually made us a little bit stupider.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:05.440
Luchins found that after giving 6 problems
that involved filling the largest jar B and

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:12.640
then taking away different amounts using Jars
A and C, the research subjects were locked

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.360
in to using all three jars to solve each problem.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.280
They didn’t even see the simpler solution.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:30.480
The final problem, 28, 76, and 3, with a goal
of 25, could only be solved with the two smaller

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.120
jars, 28 minus 3 -- you can’t even use the
largest jar to solve this problem.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.720
Many of the subjects tried to apply their
algorithm and immediately said the problem

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.080
was impossible.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:41.840
IMPOSSIBLE.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.520
They’d gained too much knowledge.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.760
They were too experienced.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.560
They’d become so smart that they got stupid.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.840
Welcome to the Einstellung Effect.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.760
“Einstellung” is a German word that roughly
translates as “mindset.”

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.280
It describes the overall method and frame
of reference for solving a problem.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:13.120
When Luchins conducted his experiment, he
gave a second group that problem that contained

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.280
two possible solutions as their very first
task.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:23.600
Most of them instantly recognized the simpler
solution because they weren’t trapped in

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.600
the Big Jar method of thinking about the problem.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:34.720
It wasn’t that Group B was smarter, it was
that Group A had learned just enough to make

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.320
them dumber.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.680
Oh -- and some of the people who were shown
the simpler solution after they declared the

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.440
final problem was impossible?

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.240
They got mad.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.600
They got really really mad.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:55.360
In his paper “Mechanization in Problem Solving,”
Luchins wrote, “Realizing it, he is embarrassed

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:01.280
by the blindness and stupidity he showed while
solving them; puzzles how it was possible;

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.000
and feels shame at having been blinded to
such a degree, rendered so incapable of reasonable

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.200
action, of intelligent procedures.”

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:18.640
Basically, some were upset that they felt
dumb and then decided to take that anger out

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.520
on the experiment itself.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:27.920
In a footnote, Luchins explains: “Instead
of viewing their responses as stupid, some

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:34.560
of these subjects remarked heatedly that the
tasks or the instructions were stupid.”

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:42.400
They were embarrassed because they felt stupid
-- and they felt stupid because they were

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.160
too smart.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.560
They’d have been smarter if they were stupider.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.360
It’s a vicious cycle.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:57.280
The Einstellung Effect is a serious cognitive
force, and it’s so strong that when a similar

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:03.360
experiment used eye-tracking software to show
whether expert chess players could recognize

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:11.040
a simple solution after a specific series
of moves, they literally did not even see

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:11.600
it.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:15.680
Their brains wouldn’t let them see it.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.040
Kinda like how you can’t see that my face
is not flat.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.280
This is actually a concave photo of my face.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.440
As you can see when I turn it, this is not
at all a flat image.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:33.360
But when seen straight-on&nbsp;
-- your brain automatically&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.520
fills in that hollow shape and projects it

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.360
outward because it’s programmed to see faces
that way.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:48.160
Grand Illusions has a hollow-face Einstein
mask that really shows just how biased our

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.800
visual systems are in favor of seeing faces
as convex.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:08:01.200
Your brain won’t allow you to see the hollow
face for the concave shape it truly is because

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.400
its shortcut supersedes reality.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:08.080
It’s why the phrase, “I can’t unsee
it!”

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.000
exists in the first place.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:11.840
Here’s a different example.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.120
There’s an old riddle about a plane crashing,&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.760
or a Starship Enterprise,&nbsp;
crashing on an international

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.240
border and being asked&nbsp;
where to bury the survivors.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:32.480
Your brain gets so wrapped up in processing
what you know -- that a country’s borders

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.680
could be relevant in determining how to deal
with the situation, that a certain number

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:44.080
of people died in the crash, and all the other
details we’re used to thinking about -- that

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.800
it takes a minute to realize that survivors
don’t have to be buried at all.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:58.880
The Einstellung Effect has created a short-term
cognitive illusion that turns a simple, obvious

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.400
scenario into a ridiculous brain teaser.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.240
Sometimes it’s just… better to know a
lot less.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.080
In a 1999 paper called “The Recognition
Heuristic,” Goldstein and Gigerenzer describe

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:20.000
a psychological experiment asking which of
two cities had a larger population -- San

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.840
Diego or San Antonio.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:28.560
A group of Germans answered correctly more
often than a group of Americans.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.880
The Americans likely considered the populations
of Texas and California, whether they thought

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.160
the cities were growing, and a ton of other
factors.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:39.840
The Germans?

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:45.520
Well, more of them had heard of San Diego,
and many hadn’t heard of San Antonio, so

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.960
they figured the more famous city was bigger.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:49.600
The end.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:50.320
That’s it.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:56.000
The “less is more effect” actually made
them more likely to be right.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:03.120
And we’ve just recently figured out that
being dumb can make you smart, right?

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:03.840
Wrong!

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.760
We’ve actually known about this problem
for a really long time.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:15.040
In 1620, Francis Bacon wrote in Novum Organum
that, “The human understanding when it has

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:20.960
once adopted an opinion (either as being the
received opinion or as being agreeable to

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:26.720
itself) draws all things else to support and
agree with it.”

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.480
Bacon didn’t know the cognitive science
behind that phenomenon, but he had the basic

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:39.440
idea right: for efficiency’s sake, your
brain tends to process new things by drawing

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.080
on the knowledge it currently possesses.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.240
It’s why I can’t make this egg stand up.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:47.760
Come on, egg.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:49.440
Work with me here.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:50.880
Please stand.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.800
The task here is to balance this egg on one
of its ends.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.920
And uh it's not really working out for me
too well.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:08.000
In Girolamo Benzoni’s 1565 “History of
the New World,” he wrote that Christopher

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:14.320
Columbus challenged a group of noble Spaniards:
"Gentlemen, I will lay a wager with any of

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:21.440
you, that you will not make this egg stand
up as I will, naked and without anything at

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:21.840
all."

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.200
I don't know why he had to be naked that's
kinda weird.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:30.080
They tried and failed, just like I’ve been
trying and failing.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:37.440
And then Columbus just kinda smashed the egg
on the table and look it stood up.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.200
He wasn’t trapped by Einstellung.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:47.520
And neither was Alexander the Great, who solved
the puzzle of the Gordian Knot in the 4th

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:48.960
century BC.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:56.640
The knot was so complex and so intricate,
a lot more intricate and complex than this,

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:03.520
this is a pillowcase, that anyone who could
untie it would immediately be declared King

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:04.320
of Phrygia.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:09.840
He just cut it in half with his sword…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:13.520
something
like that.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:19.200
Kind of like the way Indiana Jones was badly
outmatched by an expert swordsman and then

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.440
just shot him with his gun.

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:27.760
Everyone has understood this seemingly forever,
and a recognition of the Einstellung Effect

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.880
is deeply rooted in Asian philosophies.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:37.120
In Zen Buddhism, the term “shoshin” refers
to ‘the beginner’s mind’ -- an attitude

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:41.920
of openness and eliminating the constraints
of experience and knowledge.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:50.320
Zen and Daoist meditators try to achieve the
Mushin, the state of a ‘mind without a mind’

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:53.440
free from attachments and thoughts.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:13:00.560
In Japanese martial arts, zanshin is total
awareness -- not just focusing on what you

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:08.160
know or think you know, but letting every
possibility present itself, even when it’s

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:08.660
simple.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:15.280
But this isn’t just about illusions, ancient
philosophy or solving cute riddles.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:21.440
Understanding the impact of Einstellung can
literally save lives.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:27.680
In the 1970s, a cardiologist named Lee Goldman
compiled data on heart attack cases and found

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:34.080
that taking an ECG reading and answering three
basic questions could determine whether someone

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:37.120
was actually having a heart attack.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:44.160
When this shortcut decision tree was implemented
at Cook County Hospital in Chicago, the results

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.840
were incredible.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:49.840
It beat the old diagnosis method by 70%, and&nbsp;

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:54.160
the hospital found that&nbsp;
highly-trained, experienced

00:13:54.160 --> 00:14:03.440
doctors diagnosed the most serious cases correctly
75-89% of the time, while the 3-step quick

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:08.640
and dirty method was right over 95% of the
time.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:13.680
Depending on a well-educated,&nbsp;
extremely-knowledgeable&nbsp;

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:17.440
professional actually made things worse.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:22.800
People died because we were too smart.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:27.920
We are obsessed with being smart and knowing
things.

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:34.480
It’s why I’m so curious about science
and math and people, and honestly… it’s

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.720
why this channel exists.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:41.040
And look, it’s important to know stuff and
have experience.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.800
And it’s important to have a ‘mechanized’
mind.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:50.400
Luchins said, “Mechanized responses have
a place in one's behavior.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:57.040
They possess the advantages of releasing one
from the bother of finding new responses to

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:00.240
recurring everyday situations.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:07.840
They equip one with precise, ready, and speedy
responses to certain aspects of his environment;

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:14.560
and they free the mind so that it can more
adequately deal with complicated tasks.”

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:15.060
YEAH.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:21.440
But he then went on to say… “... when
a habit ceases to be a tool discriminantly

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:29.520
applied but becomes a procrustean bed to which
the situation must conform; when, in a word,

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:32.640
instead of the individual mastering the habit,&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:36.960
the habit masters the&nbsp;
individual—then mechanization

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.840
is indeed a dangerous thing.”

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:48.880
Intelligence, experience, and knowledge are
freedom -- and they can also be a prison that

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:51.840
takes away your freedom to think differently.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:16:00.320
Your brain is the greatest computer ever invented…
but it can also constrain your creativity

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:02.640
and limit your potential.

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:09.440
We all want to get smarter, become more creative,
and solve bigger and more advanced problems

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:15.200
in everything from the video games we play
to the society we live in.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:23.120
There’s so much pressure to be smart -- and
it’s just... not always about that.

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.120
Sometimes you just… gotta get stupid.

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:35.840
And as always, thanks for watching.

